# Library Management System

#### Video Demo: https://youtu.be/3_nXr6052mU

## Description

Library Management System is a command-line application written in Python that allows users to manage a small collection of books. The program provides a simple menu through which users can add, view, search, edit, and delete books. Book information is stored in a CSV file, allowing the data to remain available after the program is closed and the application is run again.

This project was developed as the final project for Harvard's CS50P course. I chose to create a library management system because it is a practical way to apply several Python concepts learned throughout the course, including functions, lists, dictionaries, loops, conditional statements, input validation, file handling, and the use of Python's built-in `csv` module.

## Features

The program provides the following features:

- Add a new book with an ID, title, author, publication year, and quantity.
- Display all books currently stored in the library.
- Search for a book by its ID or title.
- Edit the information of an existing book.
- Delete a book by its ID.
- Validate required text fields and ensure that the publication year and book count contain numeric values.
- Save all changes automatically to the CSV file.

## Files

### `project.py`

This is the main program file. It contains the application's main menu and all of the functions used to manage the library.

- `main()` runs the application and handles user interaction.
- `show_menu()` displays the available menu options.
- `load_books(filename)` loads existing book records from the CSV file.
- `save_books(filename, books)` saves the current list of books to the CSV file.
- `add_book(books_list, book_id, title, author, year, count)` validates and adds a new book.
- `show_books(books_list)` displays all books in the library.
- `search_book(books_list, query)` searches for a book by ID or title.
- `edit_book(books_list, book_id, title, author, year, count)` updates an existing book after validating the new information.
- `delete_book(books_list, book_id)` removes a book from the library.

### `test_project.py`

This file contains automated tests written with `pytest`. It tests the main book-management functions, including adding, searching, editing, and deleting books. The tests also check invalid inputs and cases where a requested book does not exist.

### `books.csv`

This CSV file stores the library's book records. Each record contains the book's ID, title, author, publication year, and quantity.

### `requirements.txt`

No external packages are required to run the project. The program uses only Python's standard library modules. `pytest` is used separately for automated testing.

## Design Choices

I chose to store the book data in a CSV file because it is simple, lightweight, and suitable for a small library application. The `csv` module is part of Python's standard library, so no additional package is required to read or write the library data.

The books are stored in a list of dictionaries while the program is running. Each dictionary represents one book and contains the keys `id`, `title`, `author`, `year`, and `count`. This structure makes it straightforward to access and update individual pieces of book information.

I separated the program into several functions instead of placing all of the logic inside `main()`. This makes the code easier to read, maintain, test, and reuse. Input validation is also handled inside the appropriate functions so that invalid book information is not added or saved.

For searching, the program converts the user's query and book information to lowercase when comparing titles and IDs. This makes title searches case-insensitive and provides a more user-friendly experience.

The program also checks whether the CSV file exists before attempting to load it. If the file does not exist, the application starts with an empty library. Changes are saved to the CSV file after books are added, edited, or deleted, so the updated information is preserved between program runs.

## Testing

The project includes `test_project.py`, which contains pytest tests for four custom functions: `add_book`, `search_book`, `edit_book`, and `delete_book`.

The tests verify successful operations as well as invalid inputs and cases where a requested book does not exist. Running `pytest` successfully passes all four test functions.

## How to Run

Make sure Python is installed and that `project.py`, `books.csv`, and `test_project.py` are in the same directory.

Run the application:

```bash
python project.py
```

Run the automated tests:

```bash
python -m pytest -q
```

Install `pytest` if it is not already available:

```bash
python -m pip install pytest
```

The application itself uses only Python's standard library.

```bash
python project.py